# Summary of changes for run 53ba5b42-3b6c-4125-a528-72b225059e54
I wrote and published the five files you asked for. They draw only on the two theory files already in the project (`Catalog/Novelty/RhoDetectorBoundary.lean` and `Catalog/Novelty/RhoAutomorphismCliff.lean`). I did not rebuild those two files: one of them imports a module that isn't in this repository, so I took your note that they compile with no gaps as given. All Python ran. The LaTeX file has not been compiled, because no working LaTeX installation was available here.

- **`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,200 words): a popular-science article built around Pollard's rho method. It explains the counted cost (multiplications) versus the uncounted cost (gcds/comparisons), Brent's real but smaller-than-advertised win (26.7% fewer multiplications, 13.8% on the wall clock), the local-win law, and the \(\gcd(E,p-1)=1\) cliff behind the 104.9× slowdown at \(p=65537\). Each theorem is stated inline in plain language.
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 5,000 words): abstract, definitions, and every result with a proof sketch:
  - the collision law for rho-shaped sequences;
  - exactly where Floyd and Brent stop;
  - Brent always uses fewer map evaluations, \(B+1\le 2F+\lambda\le 3F\);
  - Floyd never uses more comparisons than Brent, \(F\le B\);
  - the wall-clock comparison as an exact identity, and the counterexample showing it is sharp (pure cycles of length \(2^m+1\));
  - on a permutation the orbit returns to its start, and Floyd stops exactly at the cycle length;
  - the cliff criterion, the \((p-1)/d+2\) bound when the exponent shares a factor with \(p-1\), the \(x\mapsto x+1\) map taking exactly \(p\) steps, and the \(p=65537\) case.

  It also covers the experimental tables and checks, hazards, algorithms, discussion, future work and references. None of the three documents mentions formal tooling, file paths or internal identifiers; I searched them to confirm.
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`**: an `amsart` version with every theorem in theorem environments. It is shorter than the Markdown paper (about 2,500 words): all theorems and the experimental results are there, but some explanatory prose is condensed. Braces and environments match up.
- **`demo.py`**: standard library only, runs in about 2 seconds. It checks 22,650 rho shapes against the closed forms (with direct simulation as a cross-check), tabulates the cycles where Floyd wins on the clock, and confirms the cliff criterion on 540 (prime, exponent) pairs. It also reruns the closure-time experiment at \(p=65537\) (cubic/quadratic ratio about 101×), factors 300 semiprimes counting multiplications and gcds separately, and checks the birthday constant. These are illustrations; the textbook Brent variant gives different constants from your tuned measurements.
- **`PACKAGE.json`**: every schema field is filled in, with the documents themselves embedded (not filenames). It also contains:
  - three algorithms with pseudocode and Python;
  - two plotting scripts, which need `matplotlib` (not installed here, so they were syntax-checked but not run);
  - two interactive HTML widgets: a Brent-vs-Floyd cost explorer with a wall-clock map over all shapes, and a cliff explorer that draws the map's graph for small primes;
  - a guided-tour page with collapsible proofs and links;
  - your future directions, lightly edited, with the cut-off JSON fragment at the end restated as prose;
  - the full source of both theory files and their paths.

Wording choices in the papers:
- The measured gcd count fell with Brent (−14.7%), while the simple model has Brent doing *more* comparisons. I explain this as the measured implementations scheduling gcds differently from the model, which is a plausible reading rather than something the inputs establish. Either way, gcds do not fall in step with multiplications.
- The theorems are presented as proved results, and the experimental constants explicitly as measurements.
- The Bridy–Garton reference is mentioned without a full citation, because I could not confirm its details.
